    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 6281.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 755,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Three States)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard, free-flowing water we drink, swim in, and use for rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which forms at or below 0°C (32°F). \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the form of an invisible gas in the air or steam from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Unique (Superpowers of Chemistry)\nScientists consider water to be a magical molecule because of its unusual and life-sustaining properties:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a carrier, transporting vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes—whether through our bloodstream or through the soil to plants.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick together (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and they like to stick to other things (adhesion—which is how water climbs up the roots of giant trees).\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different. When it freezes into ice, it expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life. Because ice floats, it forms an insulating layer on top, protecting the life below.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the temperature of the entire planet, and why coastal areas have milder climates than inland areas.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nAs far as science knows, **life cannot exist without water.** \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. Our brains are roughly 73% water, and our lungs are about 83%. It regulates our body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects organs.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species, from the deepest ocean trenches to freshwater rivers and rain forests.\n\n### 4. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n*   The vast majority of it (about 96.5%) is **saltwater** found in the oceans.\n*   Only about 2.5% is **freshwater**.\n*   Of that freshwater, most is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and accessible aquifers).\n\n### Summary\nWater is the lifeblood of our planet. It shapes our geography, drives our weather systems, makes up the majority of our bodies, and is the fundamental requirement for all known biological life."
